- How does endianness affect input from memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit reasoning, non-branching logic, and careful boundary handling across hi/lo halves. The value is a power of two when exactly one bit in the 128-bit space is set.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value split into hi/lo; treat as two 64-bit lanes.
- Power-of-two check: a nonzero 64-bit word is a power of two if (x & (x-1)) == 0.
- Branchless composition: the single 1 must reside either in lo (and hi == 0) or in hi (and lo == 0).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch in C-like syntax (language-agnostic semantics)
int isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  unsigned int loCase = (lo != 0) && ((lo & (lo - 1)) == 0) && (hi == 0);
  unsigned int hiCase = (hi != 0) && ((hi & (hi - 1)) == 0) && (lo == 0);
  return (loCase || hiCase) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to an embedded environment with no 64-bit ops available?
- How would you verify correctness across all 128-bit values efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

