## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel popcount without loops, scales to 128-bit data, and checks careful bit-twiddling across halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR per-byte popcount
- 128-bit lane handling via hi/lo halves
- No branches, fixed sequence of masks/shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 countBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r = { .hi = b, .lo = a };
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in arbitrary 16-byte vectors with fewer operations? 
- What are the effects of endianness on byte ordering in different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of fixed-sequence bit tricks and performance constraints in bitfield work.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel summation
- Masks like 0x5555555555555555, 0x3333333333333333, 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation using BigInt for 64-bit precision
function popcount64(x) {
  x = (x) & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

